/results/' + $('#other_games_select').val();})"]] " | " [:a {:href "/event_names/75"} "Event type"]] [:h1 {:class "event_title"} "Pole Vault, Men" [:small {}]] [:table {:class "biodata"} [:tbody {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Date"] [:td {} "6 –  8 August 1984"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/300"} "Los Angeles Memorial Coliseum, Los Angeles, California"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/62818"} "19 from 13 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "The Soviet-boycott weakened the field in this event, mainly by keeping out the Polish vaulters and Soviet " [:a {:href "/athletes/77394"} "Sergey Bubka"] ", who had won the 1983 World Championships and broken the world record four times in 1984. He would have been a heavy favorite, but as his later record at the Olympics would show, that did not always count for much. In his absence the favorites were two Americans – " [:a {:href "/athletes/79142"} "Mike Tully"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/78097"} "Earl Bell"] ", and two Frenchman – " [:a {:href "/athletes/68468"} "Pierre Quinon"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/68553"} "Thierry Vigneron"] ". Quinon cleared 5.45 (17-10½) on his second attempt and then passed the next three heights. Tully, Bell, and Vigneron had first attempt clearances at 5.60 (18-4½). At 5.65 (18-6½), Quinon cleared on his first try, while Tully missed once and then passed to 5.70 (18-8¼). Bell and Vigneron went out at 5.70 (18-8¼). Quinon cleared on his first try at that height. Tully jumped after Quinon in the order and since he was behind, he passed again. Same thing at 5.75 (18-10¼) – Quinon over on his first effort, and Tully passes, trying to win the competition. At 5.80 (19-0¼), Tully missed three times and the gold medal was Quinon’s, Tully second with his 5.65 (18-6½) clearances, and Bell and Vigneron sharing the bronze medal."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th